# Thumb-Lead Melody with Brush Strums (repertoire plan: st-carter-style). An original 16-bar tune
# in C major, played the "thumb-lead" way: the tune is in the bass, on strings 6, 5 and 4 in first
# position, picked by the thumb on the beats; between the tune notes, on every "and", the index
# finger (i) brushes down across strings 3, 2 and 1 of the chord: C (x32010) G C E, F (xx3211)
# A C F, G7 (320001) G B F. Form: A (C C F C), A again (C C G7 G7), B (F F C C), A (C F G7 C).
# A tie (a dash after the string) means the tune note keeps ringing: do not pick it again, just
# keep brushing. The brushes only need finger 1 for C and G7 (string 2 fret 1, string 1 fret 1)
# and fingers 1 and 2 for F, so the other fingers are free for the tune: in F, finger 3 stays on
# string 4 fret 3 as in the chord and finger 4 reaches string 5 fret 3 for C.
title: Thumb-Lead Melody with Brush Strums
